      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, we will find the centroid of the triangle. The centroid divides each median into a ratio of 2:1. (It's also the average of the triangle's vertices.) \nThe centroid divides the triangle's perimeter into thirds, so the two perpendicular lines bisect the perimeter at points $\\frac23$ of the way from the vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.\nUsing trigonometry, we can find the height of the triangle (altitude from the second smallest side) as $\\frac9{\\sqrt{13}}$. \nWith more trigonometry, we find the perimeter of the triangle to be $14$ and its area to be $21/2$. \nWith barycentric coordinates, we can write the equation of the line that bisects the perimeter as $\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{y}{8}+\\frac{z}{9}=0$; for that line to also bisect the area, the triangle must be an equilateral triangle. \nThis configuration must satisfy the Law of Cosines with $6^2 = 8^2 + 9^2 - 2 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cos \\theta.$ $\\cos \\theta = -\\frac14,$ so the acute angle is $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\pm 4\\sqrt 5}{9}\\right)$. Its absolute value is $\\boxed{\\frac{4\\sqrt 5}{9}}$. The answer is \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{9}",
